"block_threshold":0.9,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Name the concrete privilege precondition under which bwrap's unprivileged user namespace succeeds in the chosen automated gating environment (e.g. seccomp/AppArmor profile, --security-opt, or runner type), or route it through a SPIKE that confirms the three-probe enforcement test PASSES (not skips) there, rather than relying on the one-off manual ubuntu:24.04 measurement.","cohort":["T3"],"criteria":["T3"],"decision":"block","decision_margin":0.1,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan premise: 'GitHub-hosted ubuntu-latest runners ship bubblewrap but DENY unprivileged user namespaces (setting up uid map: Permission denied), so the functional probe correctly returns None there'","scripts/mutation_sandbox.py:151-171 `_bwrap_works` requires `bwrap --ro-bind / / -- /bin/true` to return 0 — i.e. namespace creation must succeed, which is exactly what the hosted host denies","AC-4 offers 'a container image or an equivalent locally-runnable environment' but does not state the userns/seccomp precondition that makes bwrap enforce under automation","Environment-precondition axis: a platform that denies unprivileged userns is a CONTRADICTED signal for bwrap enforcement; the plan does not show the new lane's environment is not similarly contradicted"],"finding":"The plan's core new capability — an AUTOMATED, gating environment in which bwrap's unprivileged user namespace actually succeeds — rests on a single manual measurement and does not name the environment precondition that decides feasibility. GitHub-hosted ubuntu-latest denies unprivileged userns (the plan's own premise); a GHA `container:` job runs under a default seccomp/AppArmor profile that can likewise block unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER), so 'run it in a ubuntu:24.04 container' is not automatically equivalent to the hand-run that succeeded. The plan should state the concrete privilege precondition (e.g. seccomp profile / --security-opt / self-hosted or KVM runner) under which nested userns is permitted, or route it through a SPIKE that confirms the enforcing three-probe test PASSES (not skips) in the chosen automated lane before committing the full plan.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f9a40cfa016e2279e","impact":1.0,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria, AC-4 ('The mechanism is portable... A container image or an equivalent locally-runnable environment satisfies this') and Notes ('verified by hand during e668-b496 ... in a ubuntu:24.04 container ... a one-off manual measurement')","norm_id":"nde155fc6dd080bfd","priority":1.0,"reason":"high-priority+criterion-opted-in","recalled":false,"scenarios":["The new lane is built as a GHA `container: ubuntu:24.04` job; the default seccomp profile still blocks unshare(CLONE_NEWUSER), the probe returns None, and the three-probe test SKIPS — reproducing the exact silent-skip mode the plan exists to close, while AC-3 ('a skipped test is a FAILURE') then reddens the lane with no path to green"],"severity":"critical","suggested_fix":"","tier":"LLM","validity":1.0,"verification":{"binary":{"absence_confirmed_in_context":"yes","asserted_capability_confirmed":"yes","cited_reference_accurate":"yes","claims_absence":"yes","committed_work_relies_on_unbacked_claim":"yes","current_state_satisfies_plan_goal":"no","evidence_entails_finding":"yes","impact_follows_necessarily":"yes","is_verifiable":"yes","no_existing_mitigation":"yes","no_viable_alternative_explanation":"yes","path_reachable":"yes","prerequisite_attribution_valid":"na","respects_artifact_altitude":"yes","severity_claim_justified":"yes"},"severity_attributes":{"ac_unverifiable":"underspecified_oracle","blast_radius":"local","debt_impact":"high","divergent_implementation":"none","dod_uncertifiable":"certification_cannot_prove","internal_conflict":"none","irreversible_without_rationale":"none","likelihood":"high","prod_impact":"none","reversibility":"moderate","silent_vs_self_revealing":"silent","undecomposed":"no_executable_breakdown","vague_directive":"high"}}},{"block_threshold":0.7,"blocking_enabled":true,"checklist_item":"- [ ] Reference the known root cause (Ubuntu 23.10/24.04+ AppArmor unprivileged-userns restriction) and at least one established remedy for enabling bwrap namespacing in CI (e.g. a container with kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0, a custom AppArmor bwrap profile, or setuid bwrap) so the chosen environment provably lets the probe succeed.","cohort":["T1"],"criteria":["T1"],"decision":"advisory","decision_margin":-0.1044,"drop_reason":null,"evidence":["Plan Summary: 'GitHub-hosted ubuntu-latest runners ship bubblewrap but DENY unprivileged user namespaces (setting up uid map: Permission denied)'","Retrieved prior art: chainguard-dev/melange#1508 and dfaerch/bubblewrap-on-ubuntu document 'sudo sysctl -w kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=0'; katosh.github.io/agent_sandbox troubleshooting: 'Ubuntu 24.04 sets kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns = 1 by default, which blocks bwrap'; etbe.coker.com.au and asepalazhari.com describe a custom /etc/apparmor.d/bwrap profile and setuid remedies","Repo already installs bubblewrap in .github/workflows/_mutation.yml, so the missing piece is specifically enabling namespace creation, not bwrap presence","ABSENCE: the plan names no concrete mechanism (container privilege level, sysctl, AppArmor profile) despite this being the exact technical hurdle it exists to clear"],"finding":"The plan's control hinges on getting bwrap to create a user namespace in CI where ubuntu-latest denies it ('setting up uid map: Permission denied'), but it does not reference the well-documented root cause (Ubuntu 23.10/24.04+ AppArmor kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns=1) or the established remedies, leaving the load-bearing enabler of the whole plan unstated.","group_criteria":null,"group_id":null,"id":"f04c19a1775bf4543","impact":0.67,"is_primary":null,"location":"Acceptance Criteria — portabilit